  "[SCENE: Cam on desk, OWlpa3wd9K4 video cued on screen]\nWelcome to Errata. I'm Mike Hall.\nToday I want to sit down with a recording I pulled into ztranscribe:\nAlan Watts speaking to the IBM Systems Group of Engineers in the late 1960s,\n\"Seeing Through the Net.\"\n[FREEFORM: Explain why \"reaction video\" feels like the wrong phrase here.\nThis isn't about hot takes on a 55-year-old philosophy lecture.\nIt's about pausing at the timestamps where Watts is talking to systems engineers\nand noticing how squarely it lands on what we're doing with AI right now.]\n",
  "[CUE: Play / pause at 00:15:22 - 00:15:49]\n[SHOW: Transcript excerpt - \"All biological existences wiggle... some ancient fisherman one day held up his net\"]\nWatts starts with a simple image: real life wiggles.\nTo make sense of it, we hold up a fisherman's net and look through the squares.\nThe net isn't bad. You need coordinates, measurements, and categories to build anything.\nThe trap is forgetting you're looking through a net.\n[FREEFORM: Map the net to 2026 AI tooling.\nTokenizers, vector embeddings, context windows, and benchmark leaderboards are our net.\nWhen an LLM scores 90 percent on a synthetic eval, it's easy to mistake the square in the net\nfor the messy production system underneath.]\n",
  "[CUE: Play / pause at 00:17:28 - 00:18:55]\n[SHOW: Transcript excerpt - \"Prickles and Goo... gooey prickles and prickly goo\"]\nAt 17 minutes in, Watts splits thinkers into prickles and goo.\nPrickles want hard edges, discrete atoms, exact rules.\nGoo wants flow, continuity, intuition, the whole picture at once.\nAnd Watts reminds the room: nature isn't one or the other. It's prickly goo and gooey prickles.\n[FREEFORM: Connect this directly to engineering with LLMs today.\nPure \"goo\" is shipping raw probabilistic agent output without tests or types.\nPure \"prickles\" is pretending heuristics and natural language have no place in tooling.\nThe real craft is pairing deterministic harnesses, ledgers, and compilers (prickles)\nwith probabilistic search and synthesis (goo).]\n",
  "[CUE: Play / pause at 00:20:26 - 00:23:58]\n[SHOW: Spotlight vs Floodlight & The Gallbladder Specialist excerpts]\nAround 20 minutes, Watts contrasts spotlight attention with floodlight attention.\nA spotlight gives you blinding clarity on one small patch while everything around it goes dark.\nA few minutes later, he tells the story of the gallbladder specialist\nwho studies one organ so intensely that every human being looks like a walking gallbladder.\n[FREEFORM: Tie this to your Nov 7 Ramblin' & Amblin' observation about consciousness filtering out the world.\nAn LLM chat window is a spotlight. It will happily refactor a single file to perfection\nwhile breaking three upstream contracts it never saw.\nThat's why system mapping and architectural boundaries matter more now, not less.]\n",
  "[CUE: Play / pause at 00:24:32 - 00:24:54]\n[SHOW: Mechanism vs Organism excerpt]\nAt 24 minutes, Watts draws the line between a mechanism and an organism.\nA mechanism is assembled piece by piece from the outside.\nAn organism grows from the inside out, adapting to its environment.\n[FREEFORM: Contrast copy-pasted AI boilerplate stitched together under deadline pressure\nwith systems that evolve cleanly over years, keeping their feedback loops intact.]\n",
  "[CUE: Play / pause at 00:44:54 - 00:45:09]\n[SHOW: \"Confused the menu with the dinner, and we're starving on eating menus\"]\nRight near the end, Watts lands the punchline:\nwe confuse the symbol with physical reality, the money with the wealth,\nand the menu with the dinner, and we end up starving on eating menus.\n[FREEFORM: Close with what this means for engineers watching in 2026.\nDon't mistake a generated PR summary, a pretty diagram, or a wall of synthetic code for the dinner.\nRun the code against reality, check the audio, check the database, check the user outcome.\nLinks to the cleaned transcript and research notes are in the description.]\n"
]